# A generic widget taken as a blueprint value in a method's body, given a value of the
# impl's own type parameter, where the method asks for its own trait at other types that
# its own impl answers: `impl ^Framed ((a, b))` asks `^Framed (([b], Nat))`, so `a`
# becomes `[b]` and `b` becomes `Nat`. The page asks at `((Nat, Nat))`, which reaches the
# impl at `(([Nat], Nat))` and no further: a finite set of types (ruling PG; Type System
# ch. 2: recursion at another type that reaches finitely many types is legal; ch. 3: an
# impl's type parameters are decided where its head is matched; Semantics ch. 8: "a
# blueprint builds nothing until a block applies it").
# Written by Claude on the claude-lowering branch; subject to change.
emit Ui.*
test mod Tests
use Ui.*

?Pick: fn Bool, @a -> ?@a
fn ?Pick b, @w = if b: ? @w else: nil

@Named: fn a, Text -> @unit
fn @Named x, label:
    Ui.Label($text=$"{label}")

trait ^Framed a:
    ?Frame: fn a, Nat -> ?@unit

impl ^Framed ((a, b)):
    fn ?Frame ((x, y)), more = match more -? 1:
        ? less: ?Frame (([[y]], less)), less
        nil: ?Pick True, (@Named x, "framed")

@Page: @unit
@Page:
    match ?Frame ((7, 8)), 1:
        ? @t: @t
        nil: Ui.Label($text=$"none")
